<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      How the archive leaves this machine.

      `app-store-connect` is Xcode 15's spelling of what used to be
      `app-store`; 16.4 accepts both and warns on the old one. Anything else
      here — `development`, `ad-hoc` — produces an .ipa that TestFlight
      refuses, and it refuses it after the upload rather than before.
    -->
    <key>method</key>
    <string>app-store-connect</string>

    <key>teamID</key>
    <string>N2QQPW2BRJ</string>

    <!--
      Manual, after automatic was tried three times and refused three times.

      `automatic` asks Xcode to manage signing, and with an API key present it
      insists on **cloud signing** — which needs a cloud-managed distribution
      certificate. This team's certificate was created the ordinary way, so
      Xcode reported `Cloud signing permission error` and, alongside it, `No
      profiles for 'dev.supermessage.ios' were found` even once the profile
      existed and was ACTIVE in App Store Connect. It was not looking for it
      there.

      Manual signing takes both halves from the runner's own keychain and
      profile directory, which the workflow puts there: the Apple Distribution
      identity, and this profile by name. Nothing is fetched, so nothing can be
      refused.

      The cost is that the profile is now a secret to rotate before
      2027-08-31. That is a real cost and it buys an export that works.
    -->
    <key>signingStyle</key>
    <string>manual</string>

    <key>signingCertificate</key>
    <string>Apple Distribution</string>

    <!-- Keyed by bundle id, valued by the profile's *name*, not its UUID. -->
    <key>provisioningProfiles</key>
    <dict>
        <key>dev.supermessage.ios</key>
        <string>supermessage iOS App Store</string>
    </dict>

    <!--
      Symbols go up with the build.

      Without them a TestFlight crash report is a list of hex addresses.
      This is the one setting here whose absence costs nothing at upload time
      and everything at the moment somebody reports a crash.
    -->
    <key>uploadSymbols</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Bitcode is gone as of Xcode 14 and the key is ignored, but stating it
      false avoids a deprecation warning on every export.
    -->
    <key>uploadBitcode</key>
    <false/>

    <!--
      Not `upload`. This exports an .ipa to disk and stops.

      Uploading is a separate, deliberate step in `scripts/testflight.sh`,
      because a build number cannot be reused: an accidental upload is not
      undoable, it is a number burned. Exporting and uploading as one command
      makes "let me just check the archive" impossible to do safely.
    -->
    <key>destination</key>
    <string>export</string>
</dict>
</plist>
